+\section{Incremental value in a forecasting policy}
+\label{sec:policy-augmentation}
+
+The earlier residual study identifies a promising in-sample correction,
+but its comparison with a policy that excludes KAHM does not determine
+whether making that correction available improves the selected forecast.
+We therefore freeze a new policy-augmentation experiment before generating
+its cohort. It evaluates \PARepetitions\ fresh repetitions under the same
+four laws, alongside a separately specified natural-series panel.
+The design is informed by earlier outcomes; neither cohort is pooled
+with the preceding studies or used to redefine their primary comparisons.
+
+\subsection{Nested candidate menus and matched search opportunities}
+
+Let $\mathcal M_0$ be the ten-family non-KAHM menu comprising raw ridge,
+raw Nystr\"om RBF, boosting, persistence, common geometry, bounded and
+unbounded joint local-RBF departures, the common-plus-bias baseline,
+and cross-fitted and in-sample local-RBF corrections. Policy $P_0$
+selects one complete family from $\mathcal M_0$ on C. Policy $P_1$
+appends the centered in-sample KAHM correction of
+Equation~\eqref{eq:followup-fixed-common}; policy $P_0^+$ instead appends
+a centered raw-history Nystr\"om RBF residual family. Each addition
+retains the identical full-A common forecast and shared B bias.
+The added RBF family is distinct from both the complete raw-RBF
+forecaster and the local-RBF departure head already in $\mathcal M_0$.
+
+KAHM searches native caps $1,2,4,8,12$, each crossed with standardization,
+min--max scaling and $\tanh$ of A-standardized histories. Its local
+initial rank is $\min\{c,12,n_j-1\}$, followed by the native reductions.
+Cap twenty duplicates twelve at this history width; the inherited
+complete-procedure search retains the duplicate. The added RBF family
+instead crosses the same three transforms with five bandwidth factors.
+Both additions receive fifteen feature configurations and five ridge
+penalties, hence seventy-five B scores per horizon, and one added
+family in C selection. This matches additional search opportunities,
+not feature width, statistical capacity or computation.
+
+B selects the penalty separately by horizon and one feature
+configuration across horizons. C selects a correction multiplier from
+$\{0,1/4,1/2,1\}$ for each horizon, then one calibrated complete family
+by mean normalized loss across horizons. Ties prefer the existing menu.
+The same C block serves calibration and family selection, so its minimum
+loss is a development criterion rather than an unbiased risk estimate
+\citep{Cawley2010Selection}. Nested menus imply
+$\widehat L_C(P_1)\leq\widehat L_C(P_0)$, but imply no corresponding
+ordering on D. Coefficients and hyperparameters remain fixed after C.
+
+\subsection{Fresh simulation evidence}
+
+The cohort contains \PATasks\ law/noise/repetition tasks with history
+width twelve and horizons $1,4,12$. Independent episode sets retain the
+64/32/32/128 A/B/C/D allocation, with row caps 512/256/256 on A/B/C
+and all 1,664 D rows. The master seed is 2026110191; intended-distinct
+streams are checked against the previous cohorts. Losses use the
+corresponding A-target population variance. Horizons, noise conditions
+and laws are equally weighted within each of the \PARepetitions\
+independent repetition aggregates. Fresh draws extend evidence under
+known laws; they are not new application domains.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\centering\small
+\caption{Policy augmentation on fresh simulation repetitions. Entries are
+mean repetition SMSEs; lower is better. The nonlinear and linear panels
+are separate. $P_0^+$ is the added raw-RBF residual search control.}
+\label{tab:pa-policy}
